        k: 'Backtracking is DFS over a space of partial solutions with three components: choose, explore, un-choose. Its cost is exponential in principle, so all practical performance comes from PRUNING — abandoning a branch as soon as a constraint is violated or a bound proves it cannot beat the current best. Standard prunings include constraint propagation (N-queens tracking attacked columns/diagonals in sets for O(1) checks), symmetry breaking, ordering choices most-constrained-first, and bounding (branch and bound). Getting the un-choose step right is essential: mutate-and-restore is fast but bug-prone, passing copies is safe but allocates.',
