        k: 'A greedy algorithm commits to a locally optimal choice and never reconsiders. It is correct only when the problem has the greedy-choice property and optimal substructure, and the standard proof technique is the exchange argument: take any optimal solution, swap in the greedy choice, and show the result is no worse. Interval scheduling (earliest finish time), Huffman coding, fractional knapsack and Dijkstra are all provably greedy. 0/1 knapsack and coin change with arbitrary denominations are NOT — the counterexample is small and worth memorising. Matroid theory characterises exactly when greedy is optimal, which is why MST algorithms work.',
