        k: 'Divide and conquer splits input into independent subproblems, recurses, and combines. The Master Theorem gives the cost of T(n) = a·T(n/b) + f(n): merge sort (2T(n/2)+O(n)) is O(n log n), binary search (T(n/2)+O(1)) is O(log n), and Karatsuba multiplication (3T(n/2)+O(n)) is O(n^1.585), beating the schoolbook O(n²). Strassen does the same for matrices. The requirement is independence of subproblems — when they OVERLAP, plain recursion recomputes exponentially and you need memoisation, which is precisely the boundary between divide-and-conquer and dynamic programming.',
