        k: 'KMP builds a failure/LPS array where lps[i] is the length of the longest proper prefix of pattern[0..i] that is also a suffix. On a mismatch, the pattern shifts so that this already-matched prefix aligns — the text pointer never moves backwards, giving O(n+m) total with O(m) space. Rabin-Karp instead compares rolling hashes, updating the hash in O(1) per shift for O(n+m) average but O(nm) worst case on hash collisions; it wins when searching for MANY patterns at once or in 2D. Aho-Corasick generalises KMP to a trie of many patterns, and Boyer-Moore skips forward using bad-character/good-suffix heuristics, making it fastest in practice for long patterns — which is why grep uses it.',
