        py: 'def merge_intervals(iv):\n    iv.sort()                          # by start\n    out = []\n    for s, e in iv:\n        if out and s <= out[-1][1]:\n            out[-1][1] = max(out[-1][1], e)\n        else:\n            out.append([s, e])\n    return out\n\ndef max_concurrent(iv):\n    events = []\n    for s, e in iv:\n        events.append((s, +1))\n        events.append((e, -1))\n    # -1 before +1 at equal time => [start, end) semantics\n    events.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\n    cur = best = 0\n    for _, delta in events:\n        cur += delta\n        best = max(best, cur)\n    return best\n\n# O(n log n) — the sort dominates',
        q: { q: 'At an identical timestamp, why process end events before start events?', o: ['Arbitrary convention', 'It implements [start, end) so touching intervals do not count as overlapping', 'It is faster'], x: 1, w: 'That single tie-break decides your overlap semantics — the most common sweep-line bug.' }
